course: everyday
lesson: 236
title: Wind, fog, wildfire, tsunami and the seasons
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Weather changes what is safe outdoors. Strong wind can push a bike into the road or knock you off a pier. Thick fog hides you from drivers and hides the path from you.
explain: Wildfires and bushfires move very fast, especially with wind. If you smell smoke or see fire, tell a grown-up at once. If there is an evacuation order, leaving early is the safest choice, and you never go back for things.
explain: Near the coast, a long or strong earthquake can be followed by a tsunami within minutes. New Zealanders learn «Long or strong, get gone»: go to high ground or inland straight away.
explain: Each season has its own little dangers: wet autumn leaves are as slippery as ice, and in spring the lambs in the fields need peace and dogs on a lead.
explain: Example: a girl in Victoria, Australia, sees a roadside sign showing the fire danger as «Extreme». She knows it means no fires are allowed that day and her family should check their bushfire plan.
fact: In strong wind on the beach, stay away from the edge of piers and harbour walls, because big waves and gusts can knock you into the sea.
fact: In strong side winds, get off your bike and walk, especially near traffic, because a gust can push you into the road.
fact: In thick fog, wear bright or reflective clothes, walk on the pavement and cross only at a crossing, because drivers cannot see you well.
fact: When fog comes in on hills or moors (wide, open, wild hilltops), stop, stay together and follow the path or wait with your grown-up, because it is easy to get lost or walk off an edge.
fact: If you smell smoke or see a wildfire or bushfire, tell a grown-up straight away and follow the family's fire plan, because fires move very fast in wind.
fact: When there is a wildfire evacuation order, leave early with your family and never go back for toys; roads can become blocked.
fact: Fire danger rating signs in Australia, the USA and Canada show how dangerous a fire would be; at «extreme» no fires are allowed.
fact: When smoke from faraway fires fills the air, stay indoors with windows closed and do not run around outside, because smoke harms the lungs, especially with asthma.
fact: If an earthquake at the coast is long or strong, go to high ground or inland at once, because a tsunami may follow within minutes.
fact: Wet autumn leaves on the pavement are as slippery as ice, so walk carefully on them.
fact: In spring, look at lambs from a distance and keep dogs on a lead, because mother sheep may panic and dogs can chase and hurt sheep.
exercise: The wind is very strong at the beach. Where should you not go? | Near the edge of the pier or harbour wall | Up the sand dunes, well away from the water | Into the warm café by the car park | Behind a wall out of the wind
exercise: You are cycling home and strong gusts keep pushing you sideways. What should you do? | Get off and walk the bike, especially near traffic | Pedal much faster, so that the wind cannot push you | Ride in the middle of the road for more room | Ride with one hand so you can hold your hat on in the wind
exercise: Thick fog on the way to school. What helps drivers see you? | Bright or reflective clothes | Dark clothes | Walking quickly in the road | An umbrella over your face
exercise: In fog, where should you cross the road? | Only at a proper crossing | Between parked cars, where it is shorter | Wherever the fog looks thinnest | Anywhere, if you run fast enough
exercise: Fog rolls in while your family is walking on the moors. What should you do? | Stop, stay together and follow the path or wait | Run on ahead to find the way out of the fog quickly | Split up so someone finds the car first | Leave the path and take a short cut straight down the hill
exercise: You smell smoke and see a glow in the bush behind your town. What do you do first? | Tell a grown-up straight away | Go closer to check if it is a real fire | Wait to see if it goes out by itself | Phone a friend to ask what they think
exercise: Your family gets a wildfire evacuation order. When should you leave? | Early, as soon as you are told to go | When you can see the flames from the house | After packing every toy you own | Only if the neighbours leave first
exercise: You have left the house during a bushfire, but you forgot your favourite toy. What now? | Do not go back; your safety matters more | Run back quickly while the fire is still far | Ask a friend to go back and get it | Wait at the gate until the smoke clears
exercise: What does a fire danger sign set to «extreme» mean? | No fires are allowed and families should check their plans | It is a perfect day for a campfire | The fire brigade is on holiday that day, so call nobody | Only barbecues that use gas are dangerous on that day
exercise: The air is smoky from fires far away. What should you do? | Stay indoors with the windows closed | Play outside, because the fire is far away | Open the windows to let the smoke out | Run laps outside to clear the smoke from your lungs
exercise: A long, strong earthquake shakes the beach where you are in New Zealand. What should you do? | Go to high ground or inland at once | Wait on the beach for an official warning | Walk out to see if the sea goes back | Stay under a beach umbrella until it stops
exercise: What does «Long or strong, get gone» mean? | After a long or strong quake at the coast, leave for high ground | If a storm lasts a long time, go inside and close the door | If a wave looks strong, swim away from it | If the wind is long or strong, get off your bike and walk it
exercise: Why must you walk carefully on wet autumn leaves? | They are as slippery as ice | They hide spiders that bite | They are soft and make you sink | They stain your shoes a dark colour
exercise: You see new lambs in a field with your dog. What is right? | Look from a distance and keep the dog on a lead | Climb in and pick a lamb up to give it a cuddle | Let the dog run free, because it is friendly | Call the lambs over to the fence with loud whistles
test: A boy in Chicago walks to school in fog wearing a black coat. What could make him safer? | A bright or reflective vest so drivers see him | Walking at the very edge of the road | Wearing headphones so that he can hear the cars better | Crossing wherever he likes, but doing it faster
test: After a strong quake at the coast, why should you not wait for an official warning? | A tsunami can arrive within minutes, before any warning | Tsunamis only ever happen on sunny days | Warnings always come at least a whole day ahead of any wave | Earthquakes never cause big waves
test: Which is true about smoke from wildfires? | It harms the lungs, especially for people with asthma | Smoke is harmless as long as the fire itself is far away | Smoke only hurts people who are near flames | Running about outside helps clear the smoke out of your lungs
test: A family in California hears that a wildfire is coming but the road is still clear. What is wisest? | Leave early, because roads can become blocked | Wait until they can see the flames, and then leave | Stay and spray the house with a garden hose until it passes | Go towards the fire to see how big it is
test: Why should you never let a dog run free in a field of sheep in spring? | Dogs can chase and hurt sheep, and mothers panic | Sheep often attack dogs in the spring | Lambs are dangerous and may bite the dog quite badly | The farmer only minds about dogs in the winter months
test: A girl in Cornwall wants to stand at the end of the harbour wall in a gale (a very strong wind) to watch the waves. What do you say? | No, a big wave or gust could knock her into the sea | Yes, the end of the wall is the safest place in a gale | Yes, if she holds her coat tightly closed | Yes, because waves never reach the top of harbour walls
test: In mist on a mountain, a boy thinks he knows a quicker way down off the path. What should he do? | Stay on the path with his group | Take the short cut, because it is downhill | Go alone and wait for the others at the bottom | Follow the sound of the wind instead
test: Why get off your bike in a strong side wind near traffic? | A sudden gust could push you into the road | Strong wind makes bike brakes stop working | Cars drive more slowly in the wind, so it is quieter | Walking is faster than riding into wind
test: wind's crazy, waves going over the pier. can we go to the end n look | No, stay away from the edge in strong wind | Yes, the pier is safe in any wind, it's made of stone | Yes, if you hold the railing tight | Only if you're quick about it n run back
test: smell smoke outside, bushfire maybe?? | Tell a grown-up now and follow the fire plan | Go out n have a look for the fire first | Wait a bit to see if it gets worse | Close your eyes and go to sleep
test: told to evacuate, but i left my teddy upstairs! | Leave without it, never go back | Run back quickly and get it | Wait in the house for help | Ask a firefighter to wait
test: sky's all smoky from the fires far away, can i go play footy | No, stay indoors with the windows closed | Yes, smoke from far away is totally harmless | Yes, if you run fast enough to get through it | Only if you breathe through your nose the whole time
explain: A family in California waited too long to leave when a wildfire came near; the smoke and flames blocked the road, and firefighters had to lead them out.
explain: When the ground shook and the sea went out strangely in Japan, some people went to look at the beach; the tsunami came in minutes and many were swept away.
fact: Wildfires can move faster than a person can run, so families leave as soon as they are told to.
fact: If the sea suddenly pulls back or there is a strong earthquake near the coast, people must go to high ground at once.
test: sea just went way out, can we go see the fish | No, it can mean a tsunami, go to high ground now | Yes, collect the shells n the fish while u can | Yes, take a photo | Wait n see what happens with the water first
test: wildfire smoke's close, mum says pack | Leave quickly with your family when told | Stay outside to watch the fire come over the hill | Hide in the bath till it passes | Go back for your toys n games first, they matter
test: Why must you not wait when told to leave because of a wildfire? | Fire can move faster than you can run | Wildfires always stop when they reach a road | Smoke on its own is harmless to children | It will probably rain soon and put the fire out
test: did u hear a family went back for their dog's toys in the bushfire and nearly got trapped cos the road got blocked? real lesson? | Leave early when told and never go back for things | Go back if you're quick about it | Wait until you see the flames first | Roads never get blocked in fires
test: apparently a boy on the pier got blown straight into the sea by one gust in the storm, my nan saw it on the news. what's the true lesson? | In strong wind stay away from pier and harbour edges | Piers are the safest place to stand in a storm | Only small kids can be blown over | Just hold the railing tight and lean out to see the waves
test: my cousin says wet leaves on the path are no big deal, only ice is slippy. right? | No, wet leaves can be as slippery as ice | Yes, wet leaves give your shoes more grip | Yes, only snow and ice are ever slippery | No, but only in spring when the new leaves fall
exam: Identify the appropriate response to a long or strong earthquake felt at the coast. | To proceed at once to high ground or inland | To go down to the beach in order to observe the sea | To remain on the shore until warned | To swim out beyond the waves
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the speed of wildfires? | They can move faster than a person can run | They move only at a steady walking pace | They cannot cross roads or rivers | They move only during the night
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice when smoke from distant fires fills the air. | One stays indoors with windows closed | One runs outdoors to clear the lungs | One opens all windows to admit fresh air | One exercises vigorously outside for the benefit of the lungs
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a cyclist dismounts in strong side winds near traffic. | A gust may push the cyclist into the road | The wind makes the bicycle heavier | Cycling in strong wind is forbidden by law | The tyres gradually lose air when the wind blows hard
